<BaseLayout {title} {description} lang="en"><section class="case-intro"><span class="case-no">ARCHITECTURE STORY / 02</span><h1>From zero to a multi-tenant product.</h1><p class="lede">A B2B SaaS platform designed as a product from its first architectural decisions: domain, tenant isolation, security, APIs, web experience and cloud.</p></section><div class="case-facts"><div><small>Starting point</small><strong>0 → SaaS</strong></div><div><small>Architecture</small><strong>Multi-tenant · Domain · Security</strong></div><div><small>Stack</small><strong>Java · Spring Boot · React · GCP</strong></div></div><section class="case-body"><div class="prose"><h2>A product, not client forks.</h2><div><p>The domain contains organizations with different structures and operational needs. The architecture had to preserve tenant isolation without turning every customer into a separate implementation.</p><p>That made multi-tenancy, authorization and domain boundaries product decisions rather than infrastructure details.</p></div></div><div class="system-flow" aria-label="Conceptual SaaS flow"><span class="node">React</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node">API / Access</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node accent">Multi-tenant domain</span><span class="link">→</span><span class="node">Data / GCP</span></div><div class="prose"><h2>The architecture lives in the boundaries.</h2><div class="decision-list"><div class="decision"><b>01</b><strong>Multi-tenancy</strong><p>Tenant context is part of the base product model, not a later customization.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>02</b><strong>Domain</strong><p>Business rules and organizational structures modeled around the problem rather than the UI.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>03</b><strong>Security</strong><p>Authentication and role-based authorization integrated into flows and APIs.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>04</b><strong>Hands-on ownership</strong><p>Architecture, backend, frontend, cloud operations and product roadmap remain connected.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-head"><span class="label">From architecture to product</span><h2>Building from zero forces structural decisions early.</h2><p>With no legacy, every shortcut can become tomorrow's legacy. Multi-tenancy, roles, modules and domain boundaries were treated as foundational capabilities.</p></div><div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-row"><time>01</time><strong>Domain</strong><p>Processes, organizational structures, people, risks and controls shaped into a coherent model.</p></div><div class="timeline-row"><time>02</time><strong>Identity & access</strong><p>Authentication, roles and authorization integrated into the product and APIs.</p></div><div class="timeline-row"><time>03</time><strong>Multi-tenancy</strong><p>Organization separation and tenant context as part of the base design.</p></div><div class="timeline-row"><time>04</time><strong>Web product</strong><p>Spring Boot backend and React frontend evolving under one product model.</p></div><div class="timeline-row"><time>05</time><strong>Cloud</strong><p>Deployment and operation on Google Cloud Platform.</p></div></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-head"><span class="label">Engineering evidence</span><h2>Architecture is also tested where the system hurts.</h2><p>A critical Gantt overview operation moved from roughly 105 seconds to roughly 105 milliseconds for approximately 1,398 rows after optimizing data access.</p></div><div class="decision-list"><div class="decision"><b>BEFORE</b><strong>~105 s</strong><p>Approximate observed overview endpoint time.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>AFTER</b><strong>~105 ms</strong><p>Order of magnitude after optimization.</p></div><div class="decision"><b>CASE</b><strong>1,398 rows</strong><p>A concrete engineering measurement, not a general platform benchmark.</p></div></div></section></BaseLayout>